==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|mōrn|lang=enm}}, {{term|morwen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|morġen}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*murganaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*murginaz}} (compare West Frisian {{term|moarn|lang=fy}}, Low German {{term|Morgen|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|morgen|lang=nl}}, German {{term|Morgen|lang=nl}}, Danish {{term|morgen|lang=da}}, Norwegian {{term|morgon|lang=no}}), from pre-Germanic {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥kéno}}, {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥kóno}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥Hko}} (compare Welsh {{term|bore|lang=cy}} ‘morning’, Lithuanian {{term|mérkti|lang=lt}} ‘to blink, twinkle’, Skt {{term||márīcih}} ‘ray of light’), from {{m|ine-pro|*mer-}} ‘to shimmer, glisten’ (compare Greek {{term|μέρα|tr=méra|sc=polytonic|lang=el}} ‘morning’). See also {{l|en|morrow}}, {{l|en|morning}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/mɔːn/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔː(r)n|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|now|_|poetic|lang=en}} [[morning|Morning]].
#* [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s:Hamlet|Hamlet]]'', lines 165-168,
#*: But look, the '''morn''', in russet mantle clad, / Walks o'er the dew of yon high eastward hill. / Break we our watch up, and by my advice, / Let us impart what we have seen tonight

